Title: PVC
Credit: Written by
Author: Matthew Smaglik
Draft date: 09/18/2026
Notes: Short. Approx. 7 pages. All interiors.

====

INT. BASEMENT - DAY

Unfinished. Bare studs, exposed insulation and joists.

TOM (30s) comes down the stairs with a flashlight. He stops.

A white PVC pipe juts out of the concrete wall, seven feet up. Four inches across. Open at the end. Four coax cables run out of it and up into the joists.

TOM
(up the stairs)
Babe, you've got to see this.

He finds an old 12 gallon bucket, brushes off cobwebs, flips it over and stands on it, stretching to get a view.

TOM'S POV - a circle. He raises his flashlight. The beam goes in and dies somewhere in the middle of it.

He takes hold of a cable. Pulls. It comes easily. A foot of slack.

He lets go.

It slides back in. Steady. Unhurried.

It stops exactly where it started.

TOM (CONT'D)
Huh.

INT. FOYER - LATER

A dozen paint chips taped to the wall by the door. 

Indistinguishable shades of grey green. Cans of paint. A roller sleeve still in its wrapper.

Boxes, half unpacked, pushed back against the walls.

CAITLYN (30s) unpacks a box filled with pans. Behind her a trapdoor stands open in the floor. Stairs go down into dark.

Tom comes up out of it.

INT. CLOSET - LATER

Caitlyn stands in the doorway of the closet under the stairs, arms folded. Dirty with paint.

Inside the closet, Tom pulls a stud finder against the wall, it beeps. He taps with his knuckles. Taps again. Listens.

CAITLYN
That's new drywall.

TOM
It comes out of the wall tilted slightly to the right. I measured the angle. There's nowhere else it can go.

CAITLYN
Is there a version of this where you don't cut a hole?

TOM
I've gotten good at drywall.

He cuts a rough rectangle with a drywall saw. Pulls it free.

Inside: the other end of the pipe. Same four cables.

TOM (CONT'D)
I called it.

CAITLYN
You did.

She bends forward and takes a look down the pipe.

CAITLYN (CONT'D)
That's what, fifteen feet of house.

TOM
Sure.

CAITLYN
You said the beam didn't reach the end.

TOM
I'll measure it again.

He reaches and touches the cables.

TOM (CONT'D)
These must be for the wifi. Why do we have four of them.

She's already leaving.

CAITLYN
I can't wait for you to figure it out.

INT. FOYER - DAY

Caitlyn stirs paint. Tom comes through with a folded stepladder under his arm. He looks at the trapdoor.

CAITLYN
What are you looking for down there?

Beat.

TOM
I think I can hear something. Might be leaking.

CAITLYN
Can you just plug it up?

TOM
I'd be worried about mold. Can you stand at the other side while I pull on the cables? See if I can clear it.

She goes back to the paint.

CAITLYN
I've got a second coat to get on while the light's good.

TOM
Just five minutes.

CAITLYN
Five minutes.

She sets down the stir stick and heads towards the closet.

Tom sets down the ladder, lifts the weighty trapdoor, and makes his way down the stairs.

INT. BASEMENT - CONTINUOUS

Tom sets the step ladder down under the pipe. Kicks the bucket aside.

He climbs. Eye to the pipe. He lifts his flashlight.

TOM'S POV - the beam runs the length of the pipe and finds the end of it. The opening is a tiny circle of light against black. Impossible to tell the distance.

Then a sound. Not words. Low and wet. Like suction.

He pulls back.

TOM
Cait?

His voice goes in and doesn't come back. No response.

He pulls out his phone and calls. He squeezes the phone between his ear and shoulder, using one hand to hold the flashlight and the other to grip the cables.

TOM (CONT'D)
(down to the phone)
Okay. Cables. Tell me the second they move.

He draws them out, a good length of slack, coiled over his fist.

He lets go.

The first cable slides in. Steady. Unhurried.

A pause.

The second.

A longer pause.

The third.

The fourth.

Tom stares.

CAITLYN (O.S.)
Nothing.

TOM
Nothing?

CAITLYN (O.S.)
They haven't moved. Not an inch.

He looks at four cables sitting exactly where they started.

TOM
Okay. Can you look through the pipe again?

He sets the flashlight in the pipe and puts his eye back to the opening.

TOM'S POV - twenty feet. In the circle, a mouth. Only a mouth. The rest of it above the rim and out of frame.

It is smiling.

TOM (CONT'D)
Alright, I've got you. Say cheese.

The mouth holds.

TOM (CONT'D)
Okay. You can stop.

It holds.

He blinks.

TOM'S POV - the mouth fills more of the circle. Nothing about it has changed except how much of it there is.

INT. CLOSET - CONTINUOUS

Caitlyn is sitting on the closet floor beside the hole. Her face is near it but she's not looking in.

She's on her phone, scrolling through instagram, her algorithm locked in on vintage furniture.

In front of her, the four cables sit dead still in the mouth of the pipe.

TOM (O.S.)
Maybe you should tug at the cables on your end?

She glances at the pipe then back at the phone.

CAITLYN
I think the chairs got delivered. I'm going to go check.

TOM (O.S.)
Okay.

CAITLYN
Byeeee.

She hangs up the call.

INT. BASEMENT - CONTINUOUS

Tom takes the phone off his shoulder and pockets it. He lifts the flashlight out of the pipe and puts his eye back to the opening.

TOM'S POV - ten feet. The mouth is gone. An eye, wide open, turned to the wall of the pipe.

It will not look at him.

It holds.

Tom keeps the beam on it. He takes a breath and counts.

TOM
One. Two. Three.

Nothing moves.

He blinks.

TOM'S POV - the eye is closer. Still turned away.

TOM (CONT'D)
Fuck.

He climbs down off the ladder, breathes hard.

TOM (CONT'D)
Okay. Okay.

He backs toward the stairs. Gets a hand on the rail.

He stops.

CAITLYN (O.S.)
(muffled, from upstairs)
Tom? Are you still down there?

He looks at the pipe.

TOM (CONT'D)
(no hesitation)
Yeah. I think somebody's hijacking our wifi. I'm finding the box.

Footsteps cross overhead and go away.

He goes back.

The flashlight flickers as he raises it. He shakes it. It steadies.

TOM'S POV - five feet. No face. A hand, palm flat against the plastic, fingers spread, pushing itself down the pipe towards him.

In the pipe, a sound. Low. Wet. A gargling from somewhere past the hand.

Tom goes rigid.

He turns the flashlight off. Leans back, puts one hand over the pipe, blocking it.

He puts his forehead against the concrete beside the pipe and shuts his eyes.

TOM (CONT'D)
(under his breath)
It's a pipe. It's a pipe in a wall.

Sound in the dark. A drag. Then a flat knock, and another, knuckles working along plastic. Slow. Getting nearer.

His eyes stay shut.

TOM (CONT'D)
Please don't be there.

He removes his hand. The sound stops.

He opens his eyes. Turns the light on.

TOM'S POV - the face fills the circle. All of it, forced into four inches. Greasy. Stretched. Caitlyn's and not.

Too much of it for the opening.

It will not look at him.

Tom drops off the ladder.

He scrambles up. Gets the flashlight on the opening.

The pipe is empty.

He doesn't look down it again.

He works fast. Jamming rags into the mouth of the pipe around the cables. Packing it down with a broom handle. More rags over the top.

Then cord. Wound over the rags, cinched, knotted. Knotted again.

Tom folds up the ladder and takes it up with him.

INT. CLOSET - CONTINUOUS

Tom refuses to look down the hole on the closet side. He sets a fresh square of drywall over the opening in the wall. Drives the screws.

He muds it rough. Steps back.

Quiet.

Then one small tightening sound from inside the wall. A whine.

He turns off the light.

INT. BEDROOM - NIGHT

Boxes stacked along one wall. A mattress, a lamp on the floor beside it.

Caitlyn lies on her side, facing away. She reaches back without opening her eyes, drags the spare pillow over her head and pulls it down past her ear. Routine.

Tom lies on his back. Awake.

The house settles. A tick somewhere. A long groan in the joists.

Then something else. Faint. A scratch, low down, a floor below.

It stops.

Tom turns his head.

The pillow is propped on her shoulder. Between the mattress, the sheet and the underside of the pillow there is a gap. Narrow. Round at his end, going back into the dark towards her face.

He looks at it a long time.

He takes his phone off the floor. Wakes the screen. Holds it low and turns it towards the gap.

TOM'S POV - a circle. The light goes a little way in and stops. Hair. The edge of a jaw. The rest is black.

He moves the phone an inch.

Nothing comes forward. Nothing goes back.

TOM
(barely)
Cait.

No answer.

He moves the light again.

An eye. Open.

It is not looking at him.

Tom does not move.

Then Caitlyn shifts, pushes her face further under the pillow.

CAITLYN
(thick with sleep)
Turn it off.

She settles.

Tom puts the phone face down on the floor.

He lies back. He does not sleep.

INT. FOYER - MORNING

Light. Tom comes down the stairs with two mugs of coffee. He is slow. He has not changed his shirt.

He stops at the bottom.

The closet door is where he left it.

He sets one mug down on a box.

INT. CLOSET - CONTINUOUS

Tom comes through with the coffee. He stops.

A hole in the patch. Small. Punched outward, the debris on the closet floor.

He sets the coffee down. He leans in to look even though he knows he shouldn't.

Black.

He blinks.

The face fills the hole, an inch from his own.

Tom gets out. Pulls the door shut behind him and holds the handle.

Quiet.

Then, behind the door, a crack. Paper tearing. Drywall coming apart, a long ripping pull, something working its way out through it.

Tom holds the handle. He doesn't move.

The sound stops.

Weight on the floorboards. Slow. Going away from the door.

Quiet.

Tom looks up at the ceiling. At the bedroom above him.

He opens the door.

A hole in the wall the size of a person. Drywall in sheets on the closet floor, insulation, dust in the air.

In the middle of it, the pipe. Four inches across. Intact.

Nothing in it.

Tom steps in. Raises the flashlight.

The beam goes along the floor. Debris. A handprint in the dust. Another.

Under the stairs, where the ceiling comes down to nothing, something is huddled in the dark.

The beam finds it.

Caitlyn's face. Greasy. Wet hair. Folded into a space too small for her.

It turns and looks at him.

It comes. Fast. Across the floor on its hands.

CUT TO BLACK.
